brief:
The person tagged the bot in a reply. The post they tagged it in says:
@buildthis.bisks.net a single digit multiplication test to see how fast the player can do it. track answers per minute, accuracy. maybe 30 second levels? at the end show how the player compares to the actual speed of a TI-81 from 1990. also compare the speed to an nvidia rtx 3090 gpu. but be nice

The thread it's replying to, oldest first (this is the context "this" refers to):
@brennan.computer: this will perhaps poke everyone possible, but maybe the answer is that juniors just do senior stuff straight out of school

either:

- the same tool in two hands will produce different levels of output, in which case that's just 'being a junior' again

- the same tool results in similar work: fine?
  [quoting @zachweinersmith.bsky.social: Btw, even among pro AI friends, this concern is ALL OVER THE PLACE. Devs wondering how we get senior devs if the ecosystem for junior devs goes away. Doctors talking about not needing interns. Lawyers not needing paralegals. There's an ecological aspect to this and we need a plan.]
@shimmermathlabs.com: hear hear!

right now the big problem i see is schools being setup for a totally outdated workflow. it's changing but _so slowly_.
@riziles.bsky.social: I felt like memorizing multiplication tables was stupid in the eighties. It's only gotten stupider as time has gone on, and yet we still make children do it
